      <floats-group id="article-27991.s16">
        <fig id="article-27991.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Blepharophimosis Syndrome, Severe Bilateral Ptosis.&#x000a0;The image shows an infant with severe bilateral ptosis with the chin-up position. Blepharophimosis syndrome is frequently termed "BPES" for its components of blepharophimosis, ptosis, and epicanthus inverses. Other components include telecanthus and lateral canthal dystopia. There are two types of BPES: type&#x000a0;I is found in females with associated premature ovarian insufficiency leading to infertility. Type&#x000a0;II has no systemic associations. BPES is almost always inherited in an autosomal dominant manner. Spontaneous genetic mutations can also occur. Contributed by BCK Patel, MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Blepharophimosis__syndrome" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-27991.s17">
        <fig id="article-27991.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Blepharophimosis Syndrome. The arrows show blepharophimosis (A), epicanthus inversus (B), telecanthus (C), and ptosis (D). Contributed by BCK Patel, MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Blepharophimosis-components" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-27991.s18">
        <fig id="article-27991.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Blepharophimosis Syndrome, Correction of Ptosis. The image shows the correction of the ptosis with Goretex frontalis slings,&#x000a0;demonstrating the longevity of the slings. Contributed by BCK Patel, MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Blepharophimosis__ptosis__logo" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-27991.s19">
        <fig id="article-27991.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Severe Congenital Ptosis.&#x000a0;An image depicting&#x000a0;severe left congenital ptosis with poor levator function treated with a left frontalis sling. Photographs show the improved left eyelid height and how using the frontalis muscle allows the left upper eyelid to be lifted more. At rest, in unilateral cases, the ptotic corrected eyelid will invariably be a little lower than the opposite side unless the opposite normal levator muscle is removed and bilateral frontalis slings are performed (the Beard procedure). Contributed by BCK Patel, MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Left__frontalis__sling-prexpost-2" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-27991.s20">
        <fig id="article-27991.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Congenital Ptosis.&#x000a0;The image shows severe bilateral congenital ptosis with the chin-up position and poor levator function with overaction of the frontalis muscles.&#x000a0;Bilateral frontalis slings correct the eyelid position and the chin-up position. Contributed by BCK Patel, MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Frontalis__slings" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
